High-Risk Ports to Block (Inbound and Outbound)

SMB / Windows File Sharing (445, 137-139): Frequently targeted by ransomware (e.g., WannaCry).
RDP - Remote Desktop (3389): Primary target for brute-force attacks and unauthorized access.
FTP - File Transfer Protocol (20, 21): Insecure, unencrypted transfer protocol.
Telnet (23): Unencrypted remote management protocol.
SSH (22): Only allow if necessary and secure with key-based authentication.
SMTP (25): Often used for sending spam; recommend using secure alternatives (587 or 465).
SQL/Database Ports (1433, 1434, 3306, 5432, 6379, 27017): Should never be directly exposed to the internet.
DNS (53): Only allow internal DNS servers to make outbound queries, block external traffic.
SNMP (161-162): Used for management, often vulnerable
